  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nCritical fire weather conditions continue to be possible across\nthe southwest through this evening. RH values have been slow to\nlower, although there appears to be a dry line now pushing \nthrough these far southwest areas. Thus will continue current\nRed Flag Warning as breezy southeast winds are still expected\nwith these lowering RH values. Developing surface low in \nwestern MT will develop a warm front across northern and eastern\nND tonight. Some weak showers are already forming along this \nboundary, with shower and thunderstorm activity forecast to \nincrease somewhat this evening through tonight. The threat for \nsevere weather remains low, although there remains high amounts \nof MUCAPE tonight. Shear is only modest, although hodographs \nwould indicate some rotating cells are possible. Thus some near \nsevere hail is possible. DCAPE and 0 to 3 km shear are also \nincreased tonight, indicating some near severe winds are \npossible. The main threat tonight could be heavy rain, as PWAT \nvalues near 1.5 inches are forecast. Otherwise mild lows in the \n60s are expected today with a steady southeast wind. Increased \ndewpoints will be found through the night bringing high RH \nrecoveries. This could lead to some low clouds or perhaps fog \ntonight, although confidence was not high enough to place in fog\nin the forecast given the steady winds tonight. Surface low \nstalls across the area for Sunday as well as the mentioned warm \nfront. A morning upper level wave could push overnight \nconvection eastward through the morning. The stalled warm front \nwill bring another hot day with highs in the mid 80s to mid 90s.\nThis combined with continued high dewpoints will bring ample \ninstability for Saturday. Shear remains modest for Saturday, \nalthough increases Saturday evening through the night. The \nresult could be isolated severe thunderstorms Saturday through \nSaturday night. SPC has broaden the Marginal risk as a result \nfor Saturday. It may still be somewhat conditional risk, as mid \nlevel ridging and warming overhead could cap the atmosphere \nsomewhat. However, with high CAPE and modest shear, any storms \nthat do develop could be come severe. High pwats of 1 to near 2 \ninches will also bring a heavy rain threat.\n\nSurface low shifts slightly eastward and stalls Sunday and\nMonday, while influence from a broad upper trough moves in the\nstate. The result will be increased chances for showers and\nthunderstorms for the rest of Labor Day Weekend. SPC has\nmaintained a broad Marginal Risk for Sunday given high\ninstability and at least modest shear. Isolated severe\nthunderstorms could continue into Labor Day, although\ninstability is not as high. High pwats of 1 to 2 inches will\nalso remain Sunday and Monday, bringing a localized heavy rain \nthreat each day. With the stalled warm front across the state,\ntemperatures will remain mild, especially in the south. From\nTuesday to Wednesday, a stacked upper level low is projected \nover the southern Canadian prairie provinces on Tuesday before \nit quickly jets toward the northern Great Lakes and New England.\nThis favors drier, slightly cooler, and windier conditions for \nwestern and central North Dakota Tuesday and Wednesday. Tuesday\nespecially could see at least advisory level west winds. This \ncombined with some lowering of RH could bring the next near \ncritical to critical fire weather day especially in the west. \nFlow aloft may then transition to quasi- zonal by the end of \nnext week, which could moderate temperatures to near normal \nvalues and return periodic lower chances for rain.",
